WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.712
Rate limits and request controls must be validated through safe load testing and operational review.

2
00:00:06.712 --> 00:00:11.411
Local development values should not silently become production limits.

3
00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:22.217
A limit that feels fine with a few test requests may be too strict for real users, too loose for expensive endpoints, or misaligned with upstream infrastructure.

4
00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:29.735
Gateways, reverse proxies, CDNs, ingress controllers, web application firewalls, hosting platforms, and the ASP.

5
00:00:29.735 --> 00:00:32.151
NET Core app may all enforce limits.

6
00:00:32.151 --> 00:00:35.910
Teams should decide which layer owns each limit and why.

7
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.199
The edge may be better for broad traffic shaping.

8
00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:44.770
The application may be better for user, tenant, route, or business-aware decisions.

9
00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.522
Both can be useful when they are aligned.

10
00:00:47.522 --> 00:01:02.759
Monitoring signals should include limit rejections, queue pressure, latency, request body rejection, timeout trends, endpoint hotspots, tenant hotspots, concurrency saturation, backend saturation, and downstream service errors.

11
00:01:02.759 --> 00:01:08.061
These signals help teams distinguish a useful control from a misconfigured one.

12
00:01:08.061 --> 00:01:12.021
Tuning limits should not hide service reliability problems.

13
00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:26.520
If an endpoint constantly hits limits because it is too slow or too expensive, the answer may be optimization, caching, workflow redesign, capacity planning, or background processing rather than only stricter limits.

14
00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.419
Rate limiting should protect the system, not conceal poor service health.

15
00:01:31.419 --> 00:01:33.366
Exception governance matters.

16
00:01:33.366 --> 00:01:38.132
Some clients, tenants, routes, or environments may need special limits.

17
00:01:38.132 --> 00:01:43.904
Exceptions should have an owner, reason, duration, review date, and environment scope.

18
00:01:43.904 --> 00:01:50.348
Temporary exceptions should not become permanent policy because no one remembers why they exist.

19
00:01:50.348 --> 00:01:55.180
Production governance turns request controls into an operating practice.

20
00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:06.390
Limits should be documented, reviewed during releases, tested safely, observed in dashboards, and revisited as user behavior, endpoint cost, and infrastructure change.
